[Music]  when we're talking about how  leaders can manage emotions in times of  crisis  it's very important to first understand  what we mean by manage  and and what we don't mean managing  emotions means  let's let's say regulating emotions it  absolutely does not mean suppressing  emotions  the process of trying to suppress an  emotion um is something we can do  um for a short period of time um but not  indefinitely  it's very difficult uh it trades us it  takes a lot of energy  it can actually be counterproductive as  well the the act of seeking to suppress  an emotion can exacerbate it can  heighten it  can remind us of the fact that we're  feeling something and it can come  you know kind of roaring out at a time  so if we are  if we think of managing emotions as  seeking to regulate  emotions a couple of ways uh to address  that one is  learning a little bit more about our  emotional tendencies  how are we likely to respond in certain  circumstances  sometimes that's very tactical i know  that i'm about to go into a conversation  or situation  uh that i typically experience as  somewhat uh triggering  and and let me just you know sort of  prepare myself for that let me ensure  that my calendar is a little bit you  know uh clear  both before and after this particular  interaction if i've got a  particularly difficult conversation or  interaction  make sure i'm getting a good night's  sleep in advance things like that  there's also stuff that we can do in the  moment that leaders can do to to  regulate  and manage emotions more effectively in  the moment some of that simply  entails ensuring that we're framing the  the situation in a way that's going to  be most useful for our emotional  response  framing or cognitive appraisal uh  in the in the sort of term of art that  psychologists use relates to the the  meanings that we are assigning to a  given situation to a given set of  circumstances  you know am i assuming that this is  going to be a hostile interaction this  is going to be contentious  and if so what am i basing that on is  that necessarily accurate or am i just  making some  assumptions here um a more common term  that we use  uh in in my field as an executive coach  is mental models  you know we the mental models are  basically the set of assumptions  that we make about the world around us  to help us make sense of the world  and and navigate it more effectively  these models are largely accurate that's  why that's  you know that's how we get through the  day uh and they're less accurate in  certain circumstances they're less  accurate  under stress and can we step back and  actually say well what are my mental  models what are the assumptions and  interpretations that i'm bringing to  this situation  and if they're not useful can i reframe  the situation  and then we can take a number of steps  to build our capacity to regulate  emotion over time the cheesy acronym  that i use  in my class at the gsb is messy  mess if we can commit to a mindfulness  practice  and meditation is the single most  effective kind of tool in that regard  there are a number of other ways to  pursue  mindfulness going on a reflective walk  some forms of exercise  e4 exercise if we can get  some consistent regular physical  activity  s for sleep if we can get regular  amounts of sleep and sufficient amounts  of sleep and higher quality amounts of  sleep and  the second s for chronic stress if we  can identify sources of chronic stress  in our life and diminish them  we're going to be much more effective at  emotion regulation  in those moments when we when we need it  most  so under stressful conditions like those  that we're  living on a day-to-day basis in the  covenant 19 era  we often experience what's known as a  threat response or a fight flight or  freeze response  and and under those conditions one of  the one of the ways that manifests  itself  is this a sense of urgency the need to  hurry up  and although in certain circumstances  that's actually quite useful  because obviously we evolve this  response in a way to help us  survive the dilemma is that you know  very rarely these days  do we experience a literal threat to our  physical safety that requires  an immediate physical response you know  we often experience a bunch  experience a situation that we interpret  as a threat to our physical safety and  we  we speed up in in response to it and  that that  that speed that sense of urgency is  actually counterproductive  so the the impulse to hurry should i  think  often be interpreted as a signal to slow  down if we can train ourselves to  interrogate  that sense of urgency and ask ourselves  is is that really useful here  is it really going to be helpful here or  is it going to spur me to  potentially some counterproductive  action and remind ourselves to slow down  and assess what's happening  a little bit more deliberately we're  likely to navigate the situation more  effectively  i often work with professionals so the  leaders in my practice are going through  some kind of transitional phase and and  and we often have a negative emotional  response to endings this is kind of  reflected in  a line from an old tom cruise movie  cocktail  top cruise plays a bartender and in that  movie he says at one point  um everything ends badly otherwise it  would never end  and there's some truth to that because  uh you know endings  in some sense you know they're they're  all symbolic deaths  you know they remind us that this  existence will end and whatever we  believe about what transcends this  existence  we're very attached to this existence  and and it can be quite  um dislocating it can be uh you know  unnerving to be reminded of that ending  and yet i would suggest that our you  know our negative emotional response to  endings  leads us to kind of lean away from  endings as opposed to leading into them  and we thus miss opportunities to  practice to really  gain something from each ending and  learn something from it that would help  us prepare  for the much larger series of endings  that we will encounter over the rest of  our lives  i'm reminded of a line from nietzsche  that also seems very relevant here the  end of a melody is not its goal  nonetheless had the melody not reached  its end it would not have attained its  goal either  and i think that's true for every  experience we find ourselves in it's  true for the course that i teach  you know the end of the class is not the  point but if we hadn't reached  the end you know we wouldn't have gained  the experience it's true for the  students that i work with  you know the the end of graduate school  is not the point but if they didn't  reach the end they wouldn't have  achieved the goal and i think we can  apply that in a much larger  philosophical or even spiritual sense  to our lives obviously you know the  point of a life  is not to reach the ending and yet if we  hadn't reached the end if this  if this existence weren't finite in some  sense i don't believe that it would have  the same  sense of meaning so i believe that it's  important for us to acknowledge  the finite nature of all experience and  in some sense  you know recognize and lean into the  very uncomfortable  unnerving emotions that that stirs up in  part  because it's good practice it helps us  extract something  that we can learn from you know it helps  us learn something from every experience  that ends  in a way of preparing us for the larger  endings that are to come  [Music]  you

